export const DATA_05: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-005", num: 5, title: "Đề 005", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Growth of Homeschooling", paragraphs: ["Homeschooling was once seen as an unusual choice made by only a small number of families. Today, it has grown into a mainstream option that millions of parents around the world consider seriously before their children reach school age. The basic idea is simple: instead of sending a child to a public or private school, parents take on the role of teacher themselves, often with help from outside tutors, online classes, or community groups. Government education offices in several countries now report that homeschooling numbers have more than doubled over the past two decades, a shift too large to ignore.", "Parents choose homeschooling for many different reasons, and these reasons often overlap. Some families want more control over what their children learn, especially when it comes to religious teaching or a particular set of moral values. Other parents feel that traditional classrooms move either too slowly or too quickly for their child, leaving the student bored or struggling to keep up. Health concerns matter too. A child who gets sick often, or who finds crowded classrooms stressful, may simply learn better in a quiet home setting. For many households, the deciding factor is flexibility: lessons can start later in the morning, pause for a family trip, or speed up when a child shows sudden interest in a subject.", "Homeschooling today looks quite different from how it looked thirty years back, largely due to new support networks. Online platforms now offer full courses in mathematics, science, and language arts, complete with videos, quizzes, and grading tools that once required a trained classroom teacher. Local homeschool groups, sometimes called co-ops, let several families combine their efforts, taking turns teaching subjects they know well and organizing shared field trips. Libraries and museums in many cities have also begun to design programs specifically for homeschooled students, offering discounted classes on weekday afternoons when public schools are still in session.", "Despite these advantages, homeschooling still faces real and persistent criticism from outside observers. The most common worry concerns socialization, since children without daily contact with large groups of classmates may find it harder to build friendships later in life. Cost is another issue, because one parent often must reduce paid work hours to manage daily lessons, even when online materials themselves are free or cheap. Teaching quality can vary widely too. A parent confident in reading and writing might feel far less prepared to guide a teenager through advanced physics or calculus, and not every family can afford a private tutor to fill that gap.", "Even with these challenges, most researchers who study homeschooling agree that its rapid growth is unlikely to slow down anytime soon. Surveys suggest that satisfaction among homeschooling families remains consistently high across different regions and income levels. Many parents describe the daily flexibility as worth the extra effort it demands from the whole household. As online tools keep improving and communities build stronger support networks, homeschooling appears set to remain a normal, rather than rare, part of modern education."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The wide variety of online courses for homeschoolers today", "The steady rise of homeschooling, the reasons behind it, and limits", "The serious problems that make homeschooling hard for parents now", "The false idea that homeschooling always saves families extra time and effort"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has happened to the number of homeschooled students over the past two decades?", options: ["It has more than doubled across countries in twenty years", "It has slightly increased across countries in a decade", "It has more than tripled across several countries in twenty years", "It has stayed roughly steady across several countries in recent years lately"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why might a child who is often sick benefit from homeschooling?", options: ["A bored child may also learn much better at home", "A sickly child usually needs an even much stricter daily schedule", "A sickly child should generally avoid homeschooling altogether instead of adapting slowly", "A sickly child may simply learn better at home"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do libraries and museums now offer homeschooled students?", options: ["Libraries and museums offer free weekend classes for homeschoolers", "Libraries and museums now offer cheaper classes for homeschoolers on weekday afternoons", "Libraries and museums offer cheaper weekend evening classes for homeschoolers", "Libraries and museums offer advanced classes only during school holidays yearly"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a challenge of homeschooling?", options: ["Difficulty building friendships without daily classmate contact early on", "The regular need for a parent to reduce paid hours", "Government rules that prevent families from freely choosing homeschooling at all", "Uneven parent readiness to teach advanced school subjects well enough for teenagers"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"consistently\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["steadily", "occasionally", "affordably", "reportedly"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"persistent\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["severe", "popular", "personal", "ongoing"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["discounted classes", "the support systems", "public schools", "weekday afternoons"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 5 about the future of homeschooling?", options: ["Homeschooling numbers will keep rising until public schools close", "Better support networks exist only because homeschooling growth has slowed down sharply", "Homeschooling support systems will likely expand further as demand grows", "Many homeschooling parents already feel satisfied with their current daily approach overall"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To describe how homeschooling has grown while still noting its ongoing challenges", "To explain in detail the online tools that support homeschooling", "To criticize homeschooling for the practical problems it creates for families", "To persuade skeptical readers that homeschooling deserves much wider public support"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Tourism in Sa Pa: Growth, Transformation, and the Search for Balance", paragraphs: ["Sa Pa sits high among terraced rice fields in Vietnam's northern mountains. For much of the twentieth century, the town remained a sleepy hill station, first built by French colonists in the late nineteenth century seeking relief from Hanoi's oppressive summer heat. For decades afterward, it stayed known mainly to backpackers willing to endure long, winding bus rides for a glimpse of mist-covered peaks. That quiet reputation, however, no longer describes a place now counted among Vietnam's most visited destinations, one whose annual arrivals rival those of the country's coastal resort cities.", "Few single developments have reshaped a town's fortunes as sharply as the cable car that opened to the summit of Mount Fansipan in 2016. Within a few years of its opening, visitor numbers to Sa Pa climbed past several million annually. That figure would have seemed implausible to residents only a generation earlier. Hotels, guesthouses, and homestays multiplied along once-quiet roads, while investors poured money into everything from panoramic cafés to elaborate resort complexes built into the surrounding hillsides.", "This surge has not bypassed the ethnic minority communities who have farmed these slopes for generations. Hmong and Dao households, once dependent almost entirely on subsistence farming, now earn income guiding trekking groups, selling handwoven textiles, and hosting travelers in traditional stilt houses. Some studies suggest that tourism earnings in certain villages may now exceed what farming alone could ever provide. The distribution of that income across different families, though, remains far from even, since households closer to the main trekking routes tend to profit considerably more than those farther away.", "Not every consequence of this rapid expansion has been welcomed so warmly. Construction crews have cut new roads and building foundations directly into slopes that once held some of the region's most photographed rice terraces. Several of these fields have reportedly suffered drainage problems as a result. Local elders, meanwhile, describe a subtler loss. Ceremonies once performed for the community itself are increasingly staged, on a fixed schedule, for paying audiences instead. Some cultural researchers argue this shift may be quietly emptying older traditions of their original meaning even as it keeps them visibly alive on the surface for visiting outsiders to admire.", "Officials have introduced several measures meant to soften these mounting pressures somewhat. Even so, officials themselves admit that enforcement varies considerably from one commune to the next. These measures include limits on new construction near the most fragile terraces and pilot programs that route a share of ticket revenue back to village cooperatives. Community-based tourism, in which visitors stay with and are guided by local households rather than large operators, has expanded steadily, and proponents argue that this arrangement keeps a greater share of tourist spending inside the communities being visited.", "Whether these measures can genuinely reconcile Sa Pa's economic ambitions with its environmental and cultural fragility remains an open question. Neither steady visitor growth nor well-intentioned policy alone appears likely to settle it soon. What seems clearer is that the town's future will depend less on how many travelers it attracts than on how wisely that attraction is managed, a balance many former hill stations have struggled to strike."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The specific ways officials have tried to regulate new construction", "Sa Pa's tourism boom has brought gains alongside growing cultural strain", "The environmental damage that has made Sa Pa's boom unsustainable", "How every hill station in Vietnam has struggled with rapid tourist growth lately"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happened after the cable car to Mount Fansipan opened in 2016?", options: ["A cable car completed in 2016 replaced the town's hotels entirely", "A cable car completed in 2006 sharply increased Sa Pa's visitors instead", "A cable car proposed in 2016 was never completed", "A cable car completed in 2016 increased Sa Pa's visitors"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which activity is mentioned as a new source of income for Hmong and Dao households?", options: ["Hmong and Dao households now earn income from trekking", "Hmong and Dao households now earn income only from selling", "Hmong and Dao households have completely stopped all farming activity now", "Hmong and Dao households earn equally distributed income across every single family"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has happened to some rice terraces as a result of new construction?", options: ["New construction has destroyed most of the region's terraces", "New construction has been banned entirely near all terraces now", "New construction has damaged drainage in some nearby rice terraces recently noted", "New construction has improved irrigation across most rice terraces instead"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a measure taken to manage tourism's impact in Sa Pa?", options: ["Strict limits on building anything new anywhere near Sa Pa", "A nationwide cap limiting the total number of yearly visitors overall", "Programs that route ticket revenue back to village cooperatives", "Expansion of community-based tourism run by local households today"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"implausible\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["impossible", "unprofitable", "implicit", "unlikely"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"reconcile\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["balance", "merge", "expand", "recognize"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["well-intentioned policy", "steady visitor growth", "the open question", "cultural fragility"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred by connecting paragraph 1 and paragraph 6?", options: ["Sa Pa will eventually lose all its tourist appeal", "Sa Pa's challenges are not unique among former hill stations", "Other hill stations became popular only by copying Sa Pa itself", "Sa Pa was originally built as a mountain retreat by French colonists"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To detail policies now regulating construction in Sa Pa", "To argue that Sa Pa's industry should be shut down", "To celebrate Sa Pa's transformation into a major tourist hub today", "To present Sa Pa's tourism growth alongside the tensions it has created"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Influence of Sports on Young People", paragraphs: ["Although few institutions are as widely championed as organized youth sport, the actual evidence behind its supposed benefits is considerably more contested than most parents, coaches, or policymakers tend to assume. Participation rates among children and adolescents have climbed steadily across much of the world over the past three decades. This growth has been driven partly by school programs and partly by a broader cultural conviction that athletic activity builds character as reliably as it builds muscle. Whether that particular conviction actually survives close scrutiny is a separate matter worth examining carefully.", "On purely physical grounds, the case for youth sport is comparatively strong. Regular participation is associated with lower rates of childhood obesity, improved cardiovascular fitness, and better motor coordination than sedentary alternatives typically produce. These effects, however, appear to depend heavily on dosage and consistency. Sporadic participation, of the kind common among children enrolled largely to satisfy a parent's scheduling preference, may confer only a fraction of the benefit attributed to sustained, structured training regimes.", "Psychological and social claims prove much harder to substantiate with equivalent confidence. Advocates frequently credit team sport with instilling discipline, cooperation, and resilience, and some longitudinal studies do report modestly higher self-esteem among adolescent athletes overall than among non-athletes. Yet these same studies tend to note that the effect appears contingent on coaching quality and team culture rather than on athletic participation itself. That pattern suggests a poorly run team might transmit the opposite lessons just as easily.", "Rarely acknowledged with equal enthusiasm is the considerable cost that intensive youth sport can impose. [1] Overuse injuries, once associated almost exclusively with adult professionals, now appear with troubling regularity among adolescents who specialize too early in a single sport, well before their bodies have finished developing. [2] Burnout, too, has emerged as a documented phenomenon, with a substantial share of young athletes abandoning competitive sport altogether by their mid-teens. [3] This pattern often follows years of pressure to perform at a level disproportionate to a child's age and stage of development. [4]", "Access to organized sport, moreover, is anything but evenly distributed across households. Equipment, travel, and club membership fees can place competitive-level participation well beyond the reach of lower-income families in many regions. Facilities in wealthier neighborhoods routinely exceed, in both quality and availability, anything found in less affluent districts nearby. Some researchers have argued that youth sport, rather than functioning as the equalizer it is often assumed to be, may quietly reinforce the very economic divisions it is popularly credited with dissolving.", "Much of what determines whether a child's experience tilts toward benefit or toward cost appears to rest not with the sport itself but with the adults who administer it. Coaches and parents who prioritize competitive outcomes over developmentally appropriate training have been repeatedly linked to higher injury rates and to earlier dropout in the same research examining burnout. This pattern suggests adult behavior, rather than athletic activity in the abstract, may be the more decisive variable.", "None of this argues that youth sport is inherently harmful, nor does it vindicate the uncritical enthusiasm with which it is frequently promoted by advocates. What the accumulated evidence instead suggests is that outcomes hinge less on whether children play sport than on how that participation is structured, financed, and supervised. That conclusion is considerably less tidy than either sport's advocates or its critics would generally prefer to admit."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The specific physical health benefits linked to regular youth sport", "The clear dangers that make organized youth sport not worth risking", "Youth sport's benefits depend more on how participation is organized overall today", "How every youth sports program worldwide produces identical outcomes"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what determines whether the physical benefits of youth sport are fully realized?", options: ["Consistent, structured training yields more benefit than occasional participation overall", "Occasional participation yields more benefit than sustained structured training regimes instead", "Only children enrolled in professional academies receive any physical benefit at all", "Motor coordination only improves once obesity rates decline first"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is suggested about the relationship between youth sport and economic inequality?", options: ["Financial barriers and unequal facilities always close existing economic divisions", "Only club membership fees ever prevent lower-income children from playing fully", "Wealthier neighborhoods provide direct funding to lower-income sports programs nationwide every year", "Financial barriers and unequal facilities may deepen existing divisions"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as linked to coaches and parents who prioritize competitive outcomes?", options: ["A proven long-term increase in professional-level athletic achievement overall", "Substantially higher injury rates linked to virtually every competition-focused coach today", "Earlier dropout linked to pressure from coaches and parents directly", "A documented pattern connecting this coaching style to youth burnout clearly"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'For example, stress fractures and tendon damage are now among the most commonly reported injuries in this age group.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"contested\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["forbidden", "famous", "consistent", "disputed"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"substantiate\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["confirm", "exaggerate", "publish", "summarize"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["the sport itself", "developmentally appropriate training", "adult prioritization of competition", "early sport specialization"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred by connecting paragraph 1 and paragraph 7?", options: ["Youth sport will eventually be banned in most countries", "The popular belief linking sport to character may be overstated", "Character development occurs only because children play organized sport regularly today", "Youth sport participation rates have declined steadily over three decades in total"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To detail the specific physical injuries common among young athletes", "To argue that youth sport should be banned today", "To celebrate the many proven benefits of organized youth sport overall today", "To complicate the common assumption that youth sport builds character itself"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Future of Fossil Fuel Energy: Decline, Dependency, and the Contested Path Forward", paragraphs: ["Global investment in renewable generation has reached record levels year after year across nearly every major economy worldwide. Worldwide demand for fossil fuel energy, rather than contracting in tandem, has continued to inch upward in absolute terms nonetheless. That this apparent contradiction persists is less a sign of policy failure, several energy economists now argue, than a reflection of how differently decline can manifest across an uneven global energy system.", "Considered as a share of total primary energy consumption, fossil fuels have been losing ground steadily for well over a decade now. Most forecasting bodies expect this trend to continue, though not necessarily to accelerate, through the coming years. Whether this relative decline eventually translates into an absolute one, however, remains a matter projections diverge on considerably, hinging on assumptions about efficiency gains, electrification rates, and demand growth in regions still industrializing rapidly.", "Underlying this uncertainty is a set of structural dependencies not easily dislodged by policy announcements alone. Existing infrastructure, from pipelines to power plants built with multi-decade lifespans, represents sunk capital that utilities and governments alike remain reluctant to abandon prematurely . Subsidies, too, persist in many jurisdictions well past the point at which they might have been expected to lapse, sustained less by explicit policy commitment than by the diffuse political costs associated with their removal.", "Nowhere is this dependency distributed evenly. Wealthier economies, having already built out much of their energy infrastructure, can in principle retire fossil capacity faster than they add new demand, and several have begun doing precisely that. Developing economies, by contrast, are frequently expanding total energy consumption from a lower base, such that even a genuine commitment to cleaner sources may coexist, for years yet, with rising absolute fossil fuel use. Whether these divergent trajectories eventually converge remains, at best, an open empirical question.", "Financially, this divergence carries consequences extending well beyond the energy sector itself. Assets financed on the assumption of decades-long operational life may be retired early should policy or market conditions shift more abruptly than anticipated. Some analysts describe this as a stranded-asset risk substantial enough to concern institutional investors seriously and to shape long-term lending decisions. Others counter that such warnings, however plausible, have circulated for over a decade without the sweeping write-downs they implied, suggesting the risk, while real, may be considerably more gradual than its most alarming formulations suggest.", "Few questions divide industry forecasters as sharply as when precisely global fossil fuel demand might peak. Some agencies now project a peak before the end of this decade, citing accelerating electrification and falling renewable costs as decisive factors. Others maintain that any peak remains at minimum a decade away, arguing that the earlier projections underweight demand growth in industrializing economies and overweight the pace at which existing infrastructure can realistically be replaced. Neither camp claims certainty; both describe their timelines as provisional , subject to revision as new data continues to arrive.", "None of this uncertainty implies that the trajectory of fossil fuel use is simply beyond influence. It is not merely waiting to be revealed rather than shaped by deliberate human choice and sustained political will over time. What the accumulated disagreement instead suggests is that the so-called path forward remains substantially a matter of policy choice rather than a fixed destination toward which the energy system is already, irreversibly, converging."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The specific structural reasons fossil fuel subsidies persist in many countries", "The inevitable, unstoppable decline of fossil fuels regardless of any", "Fossil fuel's future trajectory remains a matter of policy choice not certainty", "How every country's energy transition will follow an identical"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the relationship between fossil fuels' relative share of energy and their absolute use?", options: ["Fossil fuels' share has fallen, but absolute use remains uncertain", "Fossil fuels' share has fallen, and absolute use is now certain", "Absolute fossil fuel use has already fallen because efficiency gains eliminated growth", "Fossil fuels' share has fallen only in already-industrialized regions"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to paragraphs 4, what may happen in developing economies as they adopt cleaner energy sources?", options: ["Developing economies will definitely keep raising fossil use regardless of", "All wealthier economies have already eliminated fossil capacity from their systems", "Developing economies raise fossil use only because wealthy nations withhold clean technology", "Developing economies may keep raising fossil use while adopting"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of forecasters' claims about peak fossil fuel demand?", options: ["A peak now projected by some agencies before the decade", "Complete certainty, shared by both camps, about exactly when the peak occurs", "A shared expectation that industrializing economies need more time before peak", "A minimum decade-long delay claimed by absolutely every single agency now"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is suggested about stranded-asset risk?", options: ["Analysts agree with complete certainty that this risk will cause massive losses", "This risk exists only because investors refuse to finance any fossil projects", "Some analysts warn of risk, though others call such warnings overstated", "Every single fossil fuel asset worldwide is now guaranteed to become stranded"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"prematurely\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["early", "late", "quickly", "finally"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"provisional\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["permanent", "popular", "personal", "temporary"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"this\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the wealthier economies", "the regional divergence", "the structural dependencies", "the stranded-asset risk"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred by connecting paragraphs 3, 6, and 7?", options: ["Fossil fuel decline will accelerate rapidly once subsidies are removed everywhere", "Wealthier economies have already begun retiring fossil capacity fast", "Political factors, more than technical limits, may determine decline's pace", "Political costs exist only because demand has already peaked in most regions"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To frame fossil fuel's future as contested and policy-dependent rather than settled", "To detail specific financial risks that stranded assets pose to investors", "To dismiss all peak-demand forecasts as fundamentally unreliable guesswork entirely", "To predict precisely when global fossil fuel demand will decline"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-013", num: 13, title: "Đề 013", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Joy of Gardening for Young People", paragraphs: ["In recent years, tending a small garden has become one of the most popular hobbies among children and teenagers in many countries. Some young people grow vegetables in a backyard, while others keep a few pots of flowers on a balcony or a windowsill. Unlike many modern hobbies, gardening does not require expensive equipment or a special skill that takes years to develop, so almost anyone can begin trying it, regardless of their age, budget, or previous experience with plants.", "One of the main reasons gardening has grown so popular is the physical benefit it offers. Digging soil, carrying water, and pulling out weeds all require real physical effort, so a young gardener often gets more exercise than they realize during a single afternoon outdoors. In addition, spending time outside in natural sunlight allows the body to produce vitamin D, which supports healthy bones and a stronger immune system, especially for children who might otherwise spend most of their free time indoors in front of a screen.", "Gardening also teaches young people lessons that are difficult to learn from books alone. A seed does not grow into a healthy plant overnight; it needs regular watering, enough sunlight, and weeks or even months of patient care before any results appear. Through this slow process, children begin to understand cause and effect in a very direct way, learning that consistent , small actions lead to visible outcomes over time. Many teachers now use school gardens to introduce basic scientific ideas, such as how plants absorb nutrients from soil or how pollination works, making biology lessons feel more real and memorable.", "Beyond individual growth, gardening frequently brings people together. Community gardens, in particular, allow neighbors of different ages and backgrounds to share tools, seeds, and advice, turning an ordinary patch of land into a shared project that strengthens local relationships. Families, too, often find that working side by side in a garden creates natural opportunities for conversation that might not happen as easily at home, since the shared task gives everyone something simple and unhurried to talk about while their hands stay busy.", "Despite these advantages, starting a garden is not always easy for every young person. City apartments, for example, may offer only a small balcony or no outdoor space at all, and busy school schedules can leave little free time for regular plant care. Even so, many schools and community groups have begun offering shared garden plots, simple container-gardening kits, and after-school gardening clubs specifically to remove these barriers, so that even students without a backyard can still experience the pleasure of watching something they planted grow into something they can be proud of."], questions: [{ q: "According to the passage, why can almost anyone begin gardening?", options: ["It requires expensive equipment and years of experience beforehand", "It is only affordable for people with high incomes", "It doesn't require costly tools or advanced skill", "It is limited to those with prior plant-care experience"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Growing plants offers several benefits despite some challenges", "Gardening mainly helps children stay physically fit and healthy", "Space and time limits mostly prevent young people from gardening successfully", "Every school should replace science classes with gardening projects"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of gardening?", options: ["Producing vitamin D in the body through sunlight exposure", "Reducing the amount of time spent on screens", "Teaching patience gradually through slow, steady results", "Strengthening relationships among neighbors and family members"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"consistent\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["occasional", "seasonal", "steady", "consenting"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"unhurried\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["relaxed", "urgent", "outdoor", "unheard"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"this\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["consistent small actions", "visible outcomes over time", "the seed's growth", "basic scientific ideas"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about schools mentioned in the passage?", options: ["Schools increasingly view gardening as a valuable part of children's education", "Schools now require every student to grow a plant in a garden", "Because students dislike science class, schools created gardening clubs instead", "Community gardens allow neighbors to share tools and gardening advice"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what have some schools and community groups begun offering to help young people garden?", options: ["Private, exclusive garden plots reserved only for wealthy families", "Costly professional gardening kits sold in stores", "Weekend gardening competitions for advanced students only", "Community plots, simple garden kits, and school clubs"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward gardening for young people?", options: ["Concerned, focused mainly on the difficulties young people face", "Supportive and encouraging, seeing gardening as valuable for children's growth", "Purely academic, focused only on gardening's scientific lessons", "Highly promotional, urging every reader to start gardening immediately"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happens to young people's bodies when they spend time gardening in sunlight?", options: ["Their bodies burn more calories than indoor exercise does", "Their skin becomes permanently protected from sunburn", "Their bodies make vitamin D, boosting bone strength", "Digging soil and carrying water tones their muscles"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Voices of Experience: Studying Abroad", paragraphs: ["Every year, hundreds of thousands of students leave their home countries to complete part of their education overseas, drawn by promises of new perspectives, stronger résumés, and unforgettable personal stories. Universities market these programs aggressively, yet the students who actually return from a semester or year abroad often describe an experience considerably more complicated than the glossy brochures suggest one shaped as much by frustration and adjustment as by excitement and discovery.", "Academic adjustment is frequently the first hurdle former exchange students mention when asked to reflect honestly. Classrooms overseas often reward open debate and independent argument rather than the memorization-heavy approach many arrivals are accustomed to, which can leave newcomers unsure how to participate meaningfully for the first several weeks. Grading systems, too, rarely translate cleanly between institutions, so a student accustomed to consistently high marks at home may suddenly find themselves earning what looks, on paper, like a mediocre result abroad.", "Where language is concerned, immersion tends to accelerate fluency far faster than classroom study alone ever could, though not always in the way students expect. Rather than steady, linear improvement, many returnees describe sudden jumps in comprehension weeks of frustrating incomprehension followed abruptly by a point at which conversations simply begin to make sense. Cultural immersion follows a similarly uneven path: small daily habits, from meal times to greeting customs, turn out to require far more conscious adjustment than the more obvious differences students had braced themselves for before departure.", "Underneath these intellectual and cultural adjustments sit more mundane, and often more exhausting, practical pressures. Housing arrangements abroad frequently fall through or disappoint, currency fluctuations can quietly erode a carefully planned budget, and the bureaucratic process of securing a student visa or residence permit is, by nearly every account, considerably more stressful than universities' orientation materials imply. Homesickness, meanwhile, rarely arrives on schedule; it may surface unexpectedly during a national holiday spent alone, long after the initial excitement of arrival has faded.", "For all these difficulties, most former exchange students, when pressed, still describe the experience as worthwhile largely because of the independence it forces on them. Managing an unfamiliar bureaucracy, budgeting in an unfamiliar currency, and building a new social circle from nothing are skills that, once acquired under pressure, tend to transfer readily into later professional life. Employers, for their part, increasingly say international experience may signal adaptability, though few claim it guarantees any particular career outcome on its own.", "Recent research complicates the tidy narrative that studying abroad simply builds better graduates. Several studies have found that self-reported gains in confidence and adaptability do not always correspond to measurable differences in employment outcomes several years later, suggesting that some of the benefit may lie more in perception than in provable advantage. Even so, few researchers go so far as to argue the experience is not worthwhile; the more common conclusion is simply that its value is harder to isolate and quantify than enthusiastic alumni testimonials tend to suggest.", "Taken together, the accounts gathered from returning students paint a picture considerably more layered than either university marketing or blanket skepticism would suggest. Studying abroad appears to reshape how students see themselves and their capabilities, even when it does not reliably reshape their job prospects in any easily measurable way, and prospective students may be better served by weighing that distinction honestly before they commit a year of their lives and in many cases, a considerable sum of money to the experience."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Studying abroad mainly creates financial and bureaucratic stress for students", "Studying abroad brings genuine difficulties alongside benefits harder to measure than assumed", "Studying abroad reliably guarantees better career outcomes for every graduate", "All university marketing about study abroad programs is fundamentally dishonest"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is often true of grading systems abroad?", options: ["Grading systems abroad have always translated very cleanly between every institution", "Overseas classrooms mostly rely on memorization rather than debate", "Only the very best students struggle with grading differences abroad", "Grading systems abroad often fail to match those back home"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a challenge of studying abroad?", options: ["A lasting decline in native-language fluency", "Visa and residence permit procedures causing considerable stress", "Difficulty adjusting to unfamiliar grading systems", "Needing conscious adjustment to unfamiliar daily customs"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"uneven\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["impossible", "cultural", "irregular", "unheard"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"isolate\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["separate", "eliminate", "research", "insulate"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"its\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["provable advantage", "self-reported confidence gains", "recent academic research", "the study abroad experience"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred by comparing paragraphs 5 and 6?", options: ["Research proves that studying abroad provides no career benefit at all", "Students' perceived growth may exceed what research confirms about career benefits", "Because employers doubt its value, students no longer feel more confident", "Employers say international experience may signal adaptability to new situations"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, when does homesickness often affect students most unexpectedly?", options: ["Immediately upon first arriving in the new country", "Whenever a planned budget unexpectedly runs short", "During a holiday spent alone, well after arrival", "When housing arrangements unexpectedly fall through"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in this passage?", options: ["To present a balanced, nuanced view of study-abroad's real costs and benefits", "To argue that recent research disproves the value of studying abroad", "To promote study-abroad programs the way university brochures do", "To warn students against studying abroad due to its risks"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does language fluency typically improve during immersion?", options: ["Through steady, linear progress rather than sudden leaps", "Through gradually adjusting to small daily cultural habits", "Only after living abroad for at least two years", "Through sudden leaps in understanding rather than steady progress"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Social Media and the Evolution of Communication", paragraphs: ["Long before smartphones existed, sociologists had already begun charting a slow drift away from face-to-face exchange toward mediated forms of contact letters, telephones, then email each shift prompting anxieties that would later seem, in retrospect, somewhat overstated. What distinguishes the social media era from these earlier transitions, however, is not simply the speed of adoption but the fact that the platforms themselves, rather than any single technology, now actively shape the grammar of everyday communication, down to how a disagreement is expressed or a relationship is publicly acknowledged.", "Message length offers one of the clearer illustrations of this shift. Character limits and disappearing-content formats have not merely shortened what people say; they appear, according to several communication scholars, to have altered what people expect a message to accomplish in the first place, favoring immediate emotional legibility over sustained argument. Whether this constitutes a genuine narrowing of expressive capacity, or simply a redistribution of nuance into other channels such as image and tone, remains a point of active disagreement among researchers rather than a settled matter.", "Perhaps more consequential than length is the erosion of traditional gatekeeping. Institutions that once decided, almost unilaterally , which stories reached a mass audience newsrooms, publishing houses, broadcast networks now compete directly with individual accounts that can, on occasion, reach comparable audiences overnight. This redistribution of reach is frequently celebrated as democratizing; less frequently acknowledged is that the same mechanism, stripped of the editorial friction that once slowed the spread of unverified claims, has proven equally efficient at amplifying misinformation, particularly during moments of acute public uncertainty.", "Alongside these structural changes, a new communicative literacy has quietly taken shape, one built as much from images, video clips, and emoji as from written prose. Rather than functioning as decoration, these elements frequently carry information that text alone would convey only clumsily tone, irony, and emotional register chief among them - such that fluency in a platform's visual conventions can matter almost as much as verbal fluency for a message to land as intended.", "Underlying all of this is the largely invisible architecture of recommendation algorithms, which determine, far more than any individual user's preferences alone, what circulates widely and what disappears unseen. Some researchers argue that this architecture has measurably narrowed the range of political viewpoints an average user encounters; others counter that the evidence for algorithmically induced polarization is considerably weaker than popular accounts suggest, and that offline sorting by geography and social class may account for just as much of the divide.", "The relationship between social media use and loneliness turns out to be similarly resistant to tidy conclusions. Early research linking heavy platform use to social isolation has, on closer examination, been complicated by later studies suggesting the association depends heavily on how a platform is used - passively scrolling correlates with worse outcomes than actively messaging close contacts does - which implies that the technology itself may be less decisive than the specific habits users bring to it.", "None of this supports either the triumphalist narrative of social media as pure connective progress or the alarmist one of irreversible social decline. What the accumulated evidence instead suggests is a technology whose effects are contingent on design choices, user behavior, and surrounding social context to a degree that resists any single, comprehensive verdict - a conclusion less satisfying than either extreme, perhaps, but considerably better supported."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Social media mainly shortens messages by imposing strict character limits", "Social media has clearly caused an irreversible decline in genuine social connection", "Ecosystem restoration shows promise, but success depends on unresolved conditions", "Every communication scholar now agrees platforms fully control public discourse"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 2 and 4, how might shorter written messages still convey nuance?", options: ["Through visual elements that fully replace the need for written language", "Through recommendation algorithms that automatically add missing emotional context", "Through disappearing-content formats that shorten what people typically say", "Through visual elements like images, video, and emoji conveying tone and emotion"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned?", options: ["Government regulators have begun directly controlling platform recommendation algorithms", "Traditional institutions no longer solely control which stories reach mass audiences", "Recommendation algorithms determine much of what content circulates widely", "The link between platform use and loneliness varies by how it's used"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"unilaterally\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["occasionally", "editorially", "universally", "independently"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"resistant\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["impervious", "hostile", "isolated", "persistent"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"this architecture\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["an individual user's preferences", "political viewpoints encountered online", "the recommendation algorithms", "offline geographic sorting"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the overall relationship between social media platforms and their users' experiences?", options: ["Platform design alone fully determines every user's social and political outcomes", "Platform design and user habits together shape outcomes more than technology alone", "Because users feel lonely, platforms design algorithms to isolate them further", "Recommendation algorithms determine much of what content circulates widely"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what remains disputed among researchers regarding shorter messages?", options: ["Whether expressive capacity has narrowed so much language is disappearing", "Whether traditional institutions still control which messages reach audiences", "Whether character limits have shortened what people say", "Whether expressive capacity has truly narrowed or simply shifted elsewhere"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall stance toward claims about social media's effects on communication?", options: ["Critical, focused mainly on how platforms enable misinformation to spread", "Cautiously balanced, resisting both overly optimistic and overly alarmist conclusions", "Purely descriptive, simply cataloguing new forms of visual communication", "Skeptical, ultimately concluding that social media does more harm than good"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 3 and 5, what do researchers disagree about regarding social media's broader societal influence?", options: ["Whether platform mechanisms are the sole cause of all societal division", "Whether emoji and video clips are replacing written language entirely", "Whether platform mechanisms mainly drive misinformation and polarization, or reflect offline divisions", "Whether loneliness depends on passive scrolling versus active messaging"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Restoring Global Ecosystems: The Challenge of the Decade", paragraphs: ["Not only have conservation biologists spent the past decade refining large-scale ecosystem-restoration techniques, but policymakers have also, somewhat belatedly, begun treating restoration as a climate strategy in its own right rather than a secondary concern trailing behind emissions reduction. Whether this shift in emphasis can deliver ecological outcomes commensurate with the scale of its political ambition, however, remains very much an open question, one that current evidence answers only partially and with considerable regional variation.", "Reforestation, the most publicized restoration technique, arguably illustrates both the promise and the pitfalls of this broader movement. Large-scale tree-planting initiatives may sequester meaningful volumes of carbon over time, but only provided the species planted are ecologically appropriate to the site and are permitted to mature over decades rather than being harvested prematurely - a condition that a substantial proportion of existing projects, evaluations increasingly suggest, do not reliably satisfy, raising doubts about whether reported sequestration figures will hold once actual, rather than projected, growth is measured.", "Wetland restoration, though it receives markedly less public attention than reforestation, may in fact offer more dependable near-term returns, given that degraded wetlands, once rehydrated, tend to resume carbon storage and flood-buffering functions comparatively quickly rather than requiring the multi-decade maturation reforestation demands; even so, the sheer cost and technical difficulty of reversing decades of drainage infrastructure mean that wetland projects remain far less common than their apparent cost-effectiveness might otherwise justify.", "Marine ecosystem restoration of coral reefs and seagrass meadows in particular presents perhaps the least settled case of the three. Early trial results have been described by some researchers as promising, yet the same researchers are typically careful to note that these results derive from small, closely monitored pilot sites rather than the ocean-scale conditions restoration would eventually need to address, and that rising sea temperatures may continue to undermine restored sites at a pace current techniques cannot plausibly outrun.", "Underlying all three techniques is a financing structure that arguably shapes outcomes as much as ecology does. Carbon-credit markets, which increasingly fund restoration projects, tend to reward measurable, easily verified outcomes tree counts, hectares planted over the slower, harder-to-quantify recovery of biodiversity and ecosystem function, creating an incentive structure that may inadvertently favor visually impressive but ecologically shallow projects over less photogenic ones whose long-term value could, in principle, be considerably greater.", "It would be a mistake, however, to conclude from these financing distortions that restoration is therefore a lost cause; rather, the more defensible conclusion the one most restoration scientists now converge on, albeit with differing degrees of confidence is that success depends less on which technique is chosen than on whether local ecological conditions, long-term monitoring, and community involvement are built into a project from its outset, a set of conditions that current funding incentives do not consistently reward.", "The decade ahead will likely determine whether restoration can mature from a promising set of techniques into a reliably effective climate and biodiversity strategy, though nothing in the current evidence base permits great confidence about which outcome is more probable, and claims to the contrary in either optimistic or pessimistic directions should probably be treated with some caution."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Reforestation alone can reliably reverse global carbon emissions", "Ecosystem restoration shows promise, but success depends on unresolved conditions", "Restoration techniques are now proven to guarantee biodiversity recovery", "Carbon-credit markets have completely ruined all restoration efforts worldwide"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 2 and 3, why does the author suggest wetland restoration may be more dependable than reforestation in the near term?", options: ["Because rehydrated wetlands are guaranteed to restore all their former functions instantly", "Because all wetlands recover faster than every reforestation project ever could", "Because reforestation causes wetlands to lose their carbon storage ability", "Because rehydrated wetlands resume key functions faster than newly planted forests mature"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned?", options: ["International courts have begun prosecuting companies for failed restoration projects", "Tree species must be ecologically appropriate and allowed to mature for decades", "Marine trial results come mainly from small, closely monitored pilot sites", "Carbon-credit markets tend to reward easily measured outcomes like tree counts"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"commensurate\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["identical", "sustainable", "proportional", "commentary"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"converge\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["merge", "agree", "fund", "diverge"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The phrase \"these financing distortions\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["community involvement requirements", "restoration scientists' differing confidence", "reforestation's carbon sequestration figures", "carbon-credit markets' funding bias"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the future of marine ecosystem restoration, based on paragraphs 4 and 5?", options: ["Its progress may be constrained by funding incentives and ecological uncertainty", "Its progress will certainly be blocked entirely by funding incentives alone", "Rising sea temperatures are caused by inadequate restoration funding structures", "No marine restoration project has ever received adequate funding"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what kind of restoration outcomes do carbon-credit markets tend to reward?", options: ["Only results that are scientifically guaranteed to be permanent", "Every possible ecological outcome equally, without any bias", "Easily measured results, like tree counts, over harder-to-quantify biodiversity gains", "Community involvement built into a project from its outset"], answer: 2 }, { q: "How does the author structure the overall argument of the passage?", options: ["By arguing that reforestation alone cannot possibly work as claimed", "By weighing each restoration technique's promise against its practical and financial limitations", "By focusing exclusively on how carbon markets distort funding decisions", "By concluding that ecosystem restoration is ultimately not worth pursuing"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 1 and 7, what does the author suggest about current confidence in restoration's long-term success?", options: ["Confidence is now extremely high that restoration will definitely succeed", "Confidence has completely collapsed among every restoration scientist", "Political ambition alone has already guaranteed restoration's ecological success", "Confidence remains limited, since evidence does not clearly favor either outcome"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
